# National Parks, Sanctuaries, and Biosphere Reserves of India

> India packs a staggering share of the world's species into a small
> land area, and protected areas are where that wealth is guarded.
> Exams love the "which animal lives in which park" pairing, the
> Project Tiger story, and counts of reserves and Ramsar sites — all
> covered here, with the year attached to every number that changes.

## 1. The Protected-Area Ladder

[definition]
A **protected area** is land or water set aside by law to conserve
wildlife and habitat. Under the **Wildlife (Protection) Act, 1972**
the main legal types are **national parks**, **wildlife sanctuaries**,
**conservation reserves**, and **community reserves**.
[/definition]

| Type | Who declares it | How strict? |
| --- | --- | --- |
| National park | State government (boundaries fixed; changes need legislative approval) | Very strict: no grazing, no private rights, no forestry |
| Wildlife sanctuary | State government notification | Milder: limited activities such as grazing may be allowed |
| Conservation reserve | State government, on government-owned land | Buffer or corridor between parks |
| Community reserve | State government, on community or private land | Local people manage it |
| Biosphere reserve | Central government programme | Whole ecosystem: core, buffer, and transition zones |

- Rough counts: about **106 national parks** and about **567 wildlife
  sanctuaries** (January 2023 figures; lists vary a little between
  sources and keep growing, so always check the year in a question).
- **Jim Corbett** (then Hailey National Park, **1936**, Uttarakhand)
  was India's first national park. **Hemis** in Ladakh is the largest.
- Under the IUCN's system of protected-area categories, national parks
  correspond roughly to **Category II** and sanctuaries to
  **Category IV**.

## 2. Famous National Parks and Their Stars

| National park | State or UT | Famous for |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Jim Corbett | Uttarakhand | Oldest park; tigers; Ramganga river |
| Kaziranga | Assam | One-horned rhinoceros; Brahmaputra floodplain |
| Manas | Assam | Elephants, pygmy hog, golden langur; Manas river |
| Gir | Gujarat | The only home of the Asiatic lion |
| Ranthambore | Rajasthan | Tigers among ruined fort walls |
| Keoladeo (Bharatpur) | Rajasthan | Migratory birds; Ramsar site |
| Desert National Park | Rajasthan | Great Indian Bustard, chinkara |
| Kanha | Madhya Pradesh | Barasingha (hard-ground swamp deer) |
| Dachigam | Jammu and Kashmir | Hangul (Kashmir stag), near Srinagar |
| Hemis | Ladakh | Snow leopard |
| Great Himalayan | Himachal Pradesh | Bharal, snow leopard; UNESCO site |
| Valley of Flowers | Uttarakhand | Alpine flowers; UNESCO site |
| Keibul Lamjao | Manipur | World's only floating national park; sangai deer on Loktak |
| Eravikulam | Kerala | Nilgiri tahr; neelakurinji blooms every 12 years |
| Silent Valley | Kerala | Lion-tailed macaque |
| Bhitarkanika | Odisha | Mangroves and saltwater crocodiles |
| Namdapha | Arunachal Pradesh | Red panda, flying squirrel |
| Sultanpur | Haryana | Birds; Ramsar site since 2021 |

[tip]
Use the nicknames: **Gir = lion, Kaziranga = rhino, Dachigam =
hangul, Keibul Lamjao = floating, Hemis = largest and snow leopard,
Desert NP = bustard.** Most pairing questions reduce to one of these.
[/tip]

Species points that exams re-use: the **hangul** is the only Asian red
deer surviving in India; the **sangai** is Manipur's brow-antlered
deer and the state animal; **Kuno** National Park in Madhya Pradesh
received cheetahs from Namibia in **2022** in India's reintroduction
programme.

## 3. Project Tiger and Tiger Reserves

- **Project Tiger** was launched in **1973**, with the first
  reserve declared at Jim Corbett.
- The **nine original reserves (1973-74)** were: Bandipur, Corbett,
  Kanha, Manas, Melghat, Palamau, Ranthambore, Similipal, and the
  Sunderbans.
- India had **58 tiger reserves as of March 2025**, with Madhya Pradesh
  and Maharashtra holding the most (six each). The largest is
  **Nagarjunsagar-Srisailam** in Andhra Pradesh (about 3,296 sq km).
- The **National Tiger Conservation Authority (NTCA)** is the statutory
  body under the Wildlife Act that oversees the project.
- The **2022 tiger estimation** counted about **3,682** wild tigers
  (the survey is repeated every four years).

## 4. Elephants, Rhinos, and Other Projects

| Project or status | Detail |
| --- | --- |
| Project Elephant | Launched in **1992**; 33 elephant reserves across 14 states (2023 atlas) |
| First elephant reserve notified | Mayurbhanj (Odisha), July 2001 |
| Elephant census | About 29,964 wild elephants in the 2017 count |
| National Heritage Animal | The elephant, declared in 2010 |
| Elephant states | Assam and Tamil Nadu have five reserves each; Kerala four |
| Project Snow Leopard | Himalayan landscape programme; flagship Hemis |

Famous elephant reserves are Singhbhum (Jharkhand), Garo Hills
(Meghalaya), Anamalai and Nilgiri (Tamil Nadu), Periyar (Kerala),
Kaziranga-Karbi Anglong (Assam), and Shivalik (Uttarakhand).

## 5. Biosphere Reserves and Ramsar Sites

[definition]
A **biosphere reserve** is a large area of land and coast that is
conserved in three zones — a legally protected **core**, a
**buffer** where limited use is allowed, and a **transition** zone
where people live and farm — under UNESCO's **Man and the Biosphere
(MAB)** programme.
[/definition]

- India has **18 notified biosphere reserves**; **13** are on UNESCO's
  World Network of Biosphere Reserves, the latest being **Cold Desert
  (Himachal Pradesh), added in 2025**.
- The **Nilgiri Biosphere Reserve (1986)** was the first, spanning
  Tamil Nadu, Kerala, and Karnataka.

| UNESCO-listed reserve | State | UNESCO year |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Nilgiri | Tamil Nadu, Kerala, Karnataka | 2000 |
| Gulf of Mannar | Tamil Nadu | 2001 |
| Sundarbans | West Bengal | 2001 |
| Nanda Devi | Uttarakhand | 2004 |
| Nokrek, Pachmarhi, Simlipal | Meghalaya, Madhya Pradesh, Odisha | 2009 |
| Achanakmar-Amarkantak | Chhattisgarh and Madhya Pradesh | 2012 |
| Great Nicobar | Andaman and Nicobar Islands | 2013 |
| Agasthyamalai | Kerala and Tamil Nadu | 2016 |
| Khangchendzonga | Sikkim | 2018 |
| Panna | Madhya Pradesh | 2020 |
| Cold Desert | Himachal Pradesh | 2025 |

**Ramsar sites** are wetlands of international importance under the
**Ramsar Convention (Iran, 1971)**. India's first two were **Chilika
(Odisha) and Keoladeo (Rajasthan)**, both designated on 1 October
1981. The list was at **101 sites as of August 2026** (it grows every
year; it stood at 98 in early 2026). **Tamil Nadu has the most sites
(20)**, and the largest is the **Sundarban Wetland** (West Bengal).
Two sites are on the **Montreux Record** of wetlands under threat:
**Keoladeo** and **Loktak (Manipur)**.

[warning]
Do not confuse a biosphere reserve with a national park. Simlipal,
Nanda Devi, and Sundarbans are *both* — a park or tiger reserve sits
inside the biosphere reserve's core. Also, **Chilika is the largest
brackish-water lagoon; Wular is the largest freshwater lake** — both
are Ramsar sites.
[/warning]

## 6. The Wildlife (Protection) Act, 1972 in Brief

- Parliament passed the Act in **1972** to ban hunting and protect
  species, habitats, and the trade in wildlife products.
- **Schedules** rank species: Schedule I and Part II of Schedule II
  carry the highest protection and heaviest penalties (the tiger and
  elephant are in Schedule I); the original Act also had a
  vermin schedule (crows, rats) and a schedule of specified plants.
  The **2022 amendment** reorganised and reduced the schedules and
  aligned the Act with the CITES treaty.
- The **2002 amendment** (in force 2003) introduced community and
  conservation reserves and stiffened penalties (at least three years
  in prison for Schedule I offences).
- Enforcement bodies include state forest departments, the **Wildlife
  Crime Control Bureau**, and the NTCA.

[example]
A question asks: "The only habitat of the Asiatic lion is in which
state?" Gir (Gujarat) is the answer. If the question says "tiger
density", think Corbett, Bandhavgarh, or Ranthambore instead. Match the
animal's nickname to the park before looking at the options.
[/example]

[summary]
National parks are the strictest protected areas; sanctuaries allow
limited use. Project Tiger began in 1973 with nine reserves (58 by
March 2025). Project Elephant began in 1992 (33 reserves). India has
18 biosphere reserves, 13 on UNESCO's list (Cold Desert added 2025),
and over 90 Ramsar sites (101 as of August 2026). The Wildlife
(Protection) Act of 1972 is the legal backbone. Memorise the species
to park pairings: Gir lion, Kaziranga rhino, Dachigam hangul, Keibul
Lamjao sangai, Eravikulam tahr, Silent Valley lion-tailed macaque.
[/summary]
